llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
119; needs 114 and 117): at one token, GATED_DELTA_NET computes its decay
and beta from the raw gate inputs, so the softplus and sigmoid kernels
before it are not dispatched.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches"). A Qwen3.6 35B-A3B decode
token with the routed experts in system RAM issues about 1,425 dispatches,
about 1,064 of them on the critical path.

Each Gated DeltaNet layer (Qwen3.5/3.6, Qwen3-Next) feeds GATED_DELTA_NET
two per-head gates: the decay g = softplus(alpha + dt_bias) * a and
beta = sigmoid(b). At one token they are one float per head (32 on
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B), yet each costs a dispatch of its own: the fused
softplus * a kernel and the sigmoid kernel. The decode kernel already loads
both values and takes expf of the decay in every lane, so computing them
there costs a few instructions per lane and no memory traffic: 60
dispatches per token fewer on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (30 DeltaNet layers).

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in
system RAM with -ncmoe 99, tg128, 5 repetitions, three alternating rounds,
HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1), patches 120 and 121
together: 24.58, 24.46, 24.57 -> 24.72, 24.78, 24.79 tok/s (+0.9%). Both
fire in all 30 DeltaNet layers of every decode token. With this patch
alone and with both, greedy 128-token output is byte-identical to the
build without them, and llama-perplexity with -ub 1 (every token through
the decode path) prints the same perplexity for every chunk. No GPU
faults in any run.

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_try_deferred_fusion runs in the evaluation loop after
ggml_cuda_try_defer_state_rows and before ggml_cuda_try_fuse.
ggml_cuda_match_gdn_gates matches a GATED_DELTA_NET with one token, one
sequence and a scalar decay per head (not KDA) whose decay input is a
RESHAPE of MUL(SOFTPLUS(alpha), a) and whose beta input is SIGMOID(b), all
f32 and contiguous, one value per head, with SOFTPLUS, MUL, the RESHAPE
and SIGMOID read only along that chain, in the shapes for which the
separate path runs the fused unary * mul kernel. All the nodes must lie
within 48 nodes before the GATED_DELTA_NET in the graph being evaluated,
so a chain split across two graphs (patch 112's head and tail) runs
unfused. The match depends only on the graph, so the SOFTPLUS, MUL and
SIGMOID are skipped when the loop reaches them, and at the
GATED_DELTA_NET the op launches a gates_t variant of the decode kernel
that reads alpha, a and b and computes
  g    = expf(softplus(alpha[h]) * a[h])
  beta = 1 / (1 + expf(-b[h]))
with the expressions of op_softplus, the fused unary * mul and op_sigmoid
in unary.cu. The snapshot store into the cache
(ggml_cuda_try_gdn_cache_fusion) and 114's state read from the cache row
combine with it as before. No node that runs between a skipped node and
the GATED_DELTA_NET may write alpha, a or b, and the op's output must not
overlap them; graph_optimize keeps alpha and b allocated until the
GATED_DELTA_NET runs. Prompt batches never match and keep the separate
kernels and 117's column kernel. GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_FUSION=1 turns this
off with the other fusions.

Numerics are unchanged: each lane evaluates the expressions of the
separate kernels in the same order, and the f32 values the separate
kernels store are the values the registers hold. On gfx803 the folded
softplus, its product with a, the expf of the decay and the sigmoid
compile to the instruction sequences of unary_gated_op_kernel<op_softplus>,
unary_op_kernel<op_sigmoid> and the kernel's own expf (same range
reduction constants, v_mac/v_mad order, v_exp/v_log, the correctly
rounded division; v_fma only inside that division, as in the sigmoid
kernel). The low half of the expf argument is formed as v_mad_f32
(a * softplus - high half), an unfused multiply-add that rounds like the
separate multiply and subtract, where the separate path subtracts from
the stored product: the same bits. The variants without gates compile as
before.

